              [1m[35mFBI reportedly investigating apparent hack of its criminal database[0m

              2 hours ago[2m[33m (https://www.google.com/preferences/source?q=bbc.com)[0m Jaroslav Lukiv

              The FBI is investigating an apparent breach of its criminal information
              database, US media report citing two people briefed on the issue.

              The National Crime Information Center (NCIC) database is used by federal and
              local law enforcement to share information on warrants and background checks.
              The BBC has contacted the FBI for comment.

              This comes after string of tech outages which hit banking, air traffic control
              and other sectors in the US last week. Officials have said they were not caused
              by cyberattacks - but the blackouts have sparked scrutiny from lawmakers.

              [4m[34mWhite House National Cyber Director Sean Cairncross was fired this week,[0m
              [2m[33m(https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/articles/cwz0k3nn5p5mo)[0m with US President Donald
              Trump saying he was not good enough.

              It would not be the first time hackers have targeted the FBI, the domestic
              intelligence and security service of the US.

              In September,[4m[34m the FBI said it was investigating an apparent breach[0m
              [2m[33m(https://www.bbc.com/news/articles/c9n74574elljo)[0m by a cyber-crime group
              ShinyHunters, which claimed it had stolen sensitive information on all bureau
              staff - around 38,000 people.

              After that hack, one former FBI agent told the BBC he feared for the safety of
              current employees, whose personal information could soon become available online
